<br/>
The announcement received a mixed response. Our <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2001/10/23/apples-new-thing-ipod/">original coverage</a> is still available, along with reader comments questioning the device's price, Mac-only compatibility, and significance. One reader described it as "far from revolutionary," while another wrote, "all this hype for something so ridiculous!" Early forum discussions also covered fingerprints on the polished rear enclosure, heat while charging, and the sound quality of the included earbuds.<br/>
<br/>
Hardware revisions followed in quick succession. The second-generation iPod adopted a touch-sensitive scroll wheel, while the third-generation model introduced the 30-pin Dock Connector in 2003. The full-size iPod gained a color display in 2004 and video playback in 2005.<br/>
<br/>
The iPod mini was announced in January 2004, representing the first extension of the product line. It featured a smaller anodized aluminum enclosure in silver, gold, pink, blue, or green, and introduced the Click Wheel, which integrated playback buttons into the touch-sensitive scrolling surface. Apple adopted the same control for the fourth-generation full-size iPod later that year.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/08/Apple-iPod-Classic-First-Generation-Sealed-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1015662" /><br/>
<br/>
In 2005, Apple introduced both the iPod shuffle and iPod nano. The shuffle omitted a display and offered a smaller, simpler music player, while the nano replaced the mini with a thin design based on flash storage. Later iPod nano models added video playback, a video camera, and a touchscreen. The square sixth-generation model, introduced in 2010, could be worn on the wrist using third-party watch straps.<br/>
<br/>
By April 2007, Apple had <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2007/04/09/apple-sells-100-millionth-iPod/">sold 100 million iPods</a>, less than six years after the first model reached customers. By <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2011/10/23/ipod-turns-10-years-old-today/">October 2011</a>, sales had exceeded 304 million units.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/14/ipod-touch-released-19-years-ago-today/">iPod touch</a> arrived in September 2007, several months after the first iPhone went on sale. It featured a 3.5-inch Multi-Touch display, Wi-Fi, and Safari, without requiring a cellular plan.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2022/05/iPod-Touch-Background-Feature-2.jpg" alt="" width="1984" height="1116" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-845343" /><br/>
<br/>
Apple discontinued the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipod-classic/">iPod classic in September 2014</a>, five years after its final hardware revision. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2017/07/27/APPLE-REMOVES-IPOD-NANO-IPOD-SHUFFLE/">iPod nano and iPod shuffle</a> followed in July 2017, leaving the iPod touch as the sole model.<br/>
<br/>
The seventh-generation iPod touch received an A10 Fusion chip and up to 256GB of storage in May 2019. It remained on sale without further hardware updates until May 10, 2022, when Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2022/05/10/apple-discontinues-ipod-touch/">announced its discontinuation</a> and said it would be available while supplies lasted.<br/>
<br/>
